  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "Calculus begins with the limit — the value a function approaches near a point — and with continuity, which guarantees no breaks in a graph.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Reference — Limits &amp; continuity",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Limit laws apply term by term. A function is <strong>continuous</strong> at \\( a \\) when \\( \\lim_{x\\to a}f(x)=f(a) \\). Discontinuities are removable, jump, or infinite. The <strong>Intermediate Value Theorem</strong>: a function continuous on \\( [a,b] \\) attains every value between \\( f(a) \\) and \\( f(b) \\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Limit",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\lim_{x\\to a}f(x)=L \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Squeeze theorem",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "If \\( g\\leq f\\leq h \\) and \\( \\lim g=\\lim h=L \\) then \\( \\lim f=L \\)",
              "ar": null
            },
            "wide": true
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Continuity",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( f \\) continuous at \\( a \\) iff \\( \\lim_{x\\to a}f(x)=f(a) \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Key limit",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\lim_{x\\to0}\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x}=1 \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Continuity",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\lim_{x\\to a}f=f(a) \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "IVT",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "attains all middle values",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 1.A",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Evaluate \\( \\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to2}\\frac{x^2-4}{x-2} \\).</p>\\[ \\lim_{x\\to2}\\frac{(x-2)(x+2)}{x-2}=\\lim_{x\\to2}(x+2)=4. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example — ε-δ and IVT",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Use the Intermediate Value Theorem to show \\(f(x)=x^3-x-1\\) has a root on \\([1,2]\\).</p><p>\\(f(1)=1-1-1=-1&lt;0\\) and \\(f(2)=8-2-1=5&gt;0\\). Since \\(f\\) is continuous on \\([1,2]\\) and changes sign, by IVT there exists \\(c\\in(1,2)\\) with \\(f(c)=0\\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "Find \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\dfrac{\\sin(3x)}{5x}\\).",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "Use \\(\\lim_{u\\to0}\\frac{\\sin u}{u}=1\\): \\(\\dfrac{\\sin(3x)}{5x}=\\dfrac{3}{5}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sin(3x)}{3x}\\to\\dfrac{3}{5}\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
